<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    NOSLOP_TLS_TRUST_V1

    The previous version of this file did two things that undercut the whole
    privacy argument:

      1. base-config set cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" for EVERY domain, so
         any plain-HTTP URL that reached the app was fetched without complaint.
      2. base-config trusted <certificates src="user" />, meaning any CA the
         user (or an MDM profile, or malware with the right prompt) had
         installed could transparently MITM every TLS connection NoSlop makes —
         feeds, the update check, the API proxy, all of it.

    Now: system trust anchors only, TLS required everywhere, and cleartext
    permitted ONLY where it is both necessary and safe — Tor hidden services
    (already authenticated and encrypted at the rendezvous layer, and no public
    CA issues certificates for .onion names) and loopback (the local media proxy
    and mesh listener, which never leave the device).

    KNOWN TRADE-OFF — LAN HUB:
    NoSlop talks to a Hub on the local network over plain HTTP at
    http://<lan-ip>:8080. Android's network security config matches hostnames
    only; it has no CIDR syntax, so there is no way to express "any RFC1918
    address" here. With this config that LAN request now fails and
    NoSlopRepository.invokeHubApi() falls back to the Hub's .onion route —
    correct, but slower. HubSetupScreen and QR link-by-IP will report the Hub
    as unreachable.

    If you want the LAN fast path back, uncomment the block at the bottom and
    put your Hub's actual LAN address in it. That is a deliberate, scoped
    exception for one known host rather than a blanket permit for every domain.

    To intercept TLS with a proxy such as mitmproxy while debugging, add a
    debug-only overlay at app/src/debug/res/xml/network_security_config.xml
    that re-enables user CAs. Do not re-enable them here.
-->
<network-security-config>

    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <!-- Tor hidden services and loopback: cleartext by design. -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">onion</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">localhost</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">127.0.0.1</domain>
    </domain-config>

    <!--
    Optional LAN Hub fast path. Uncomment and replace with your Hub's address.

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">192.168.1.42</domain>
    </domain-config>
    -->

</network-security-config>
